fr:mainmatter.fr:tex:
\begin {align*}
    S\space  n &= (\lambda  n.\lambda  f.\lambda  x.f(n\space  f\space  x))\space  n \\
    &\to _\beta  \lambda  f.\lambda  x.f(n\space  f\space  x) \\
    &\to _\beta  \lambda  f.\lambda  x.f^n(x) \\
    &\to _\beta  n+1
\end {align*}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Everything appears simply until we think of a very simple function,
the predecessor function \text {pred} defined by

    \begin {align*}
        \text {pred}\space  0 = 0 \\
        \text {pred}\space  (n+1) = n
    \end {align*}

What we would need is the schema of primitive recursion

    \begin {align*}
        f\space  0 &= c \\
        f\space  (n+1) &= g\space  n\space  (f\space  n)
    \end {align*}

With which we can define the predecessor function by 

    g = \lambda  x.\lambda  y.x

fr:tree.fr:mainmatter:
Now we need to define pairs and \text {letPair}. The idea is to simply abstract over the continuation itself.
\begin {align*} \langle x,y\rangle &= \lambda k.k\space x\space y \\ \text {pair} &= \lambda x.\lambda y.\lambda k.k\space x\space y \\ \text {letPair} &= \lambda p.p \end {align*}
The letPair is not really needed here.

Summarizing the above and we obtain the full definition of the predecessor function.

    \begin {align*}
        \text {pred}_2 &= \lambda  n.n\space  (\lambda  p.p (\lambda  xy.\text {pair} \space  (x+1) \space  x))\space  \text {pair} (\space  0 \space  0)\\ 
        \text {pred} &= \lambda  n. (\text {pred}_2\space  n) \space  (\lambda  xy.y)
    \end {align*}
